1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,630
I recently read that during World War Two,

2
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:08,950
Joseph Stalin had a research program

3
00:00:08,950 --> 00:00:10,660
to create supersoldiers

4
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:14,040
by having women impregnated by gorillas.

5
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,150
What a sick use of science.

6
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:19,300
Hey, as long as the baby's healthy.

7
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:23,560
I wonder if Stalin considered any other animals.

8
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:25,580
Hippos are the deadliest creature.

9
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:28,990
A half-human, half-hippo soldier would be pretty badass.

10
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,850
Yes, but when they're hungry-hungry,

11
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,480
you can stop them with marbles.

12
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,020
Yeah, the correct animal

13
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:38,960
for interspecies supersolider is koala.

14
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,950
You would wind up with an army so cute it couldn't be attacked.

15
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:46,650
But half-man, half-owl could fly...

16
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:49,290
The answer is cuddly soldiers with big flat noses.

17
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:50,540
Moving on.

18
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,540
So, Penny, when's the new job start?

19
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:54,720
Next Monday.

20
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:57,010
Did you get a chance to look over the materials I gave you?

21
00:00:57,010 --> 00:00:58,310
Uh, not yet, but I will.

22
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:00,380
Great. When?

23
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:03,060
I said I'll get to it.

24
00:01:06,690 --> 00:01:09,450
I'm sensing awkwardness, am I right?

25
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:10,420
Yes.

26
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:12,600
Swish.

27
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:16,540
I don't want to be pushy,

28
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:19,350
but you've never done pharmaceutical sales before.

29
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:21,850
It seems like you could use this time to get a head start.

30
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:23,450
Well, the first few weeks will be all training.

31
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:25,120
They'll tell me everything I need to know.

32
00:01:25,310 --> 00:01:27,270
But imagine how impressed they'd be if you showed up

33
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:29,180
already familiar with the material.

34
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:31,430
Okay, so what, you want me to be like a teacher's pet?

35
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,240
Couldn't hurt.

36
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:35,080
Mm, I don't know.

37
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:36,140
Who here has ever been hurt

38
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:38,110
because they were the teacher's pet?

39
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:44,060
It was like the rest of the class wanted Ms. McDonald

40
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:45,430
to forget the quiz.

41
00:02:12,860 --> 00:02:14,000
Mrs. Davis.

42
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:15,480
Guess who?

43
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:16,850
Dr. Cooper.

44
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:18,940
That's right, good job.

45
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:21,320
Hello.

46
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:23,510
Uh, you wanted to see me?

47
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:25,150
Yes. Uh, welcome back.

48
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,180
Thank you.

49
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:29,280
I assume you'd like to reopen our dialogue

50
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,200
about the university forcing me to continue with string theory?

51
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:33,800
You mean the dialogue that went,

52
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:36,740
"Please?" "No." "Please?" "No." "Please?" "No."

53
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:39,410
That's the one.

54
00:02:41,130 --> 00:02:44,380
I believe you went last, so, uh, please?

55
00:02:45,110 --> 00:02:48,760
Dr. Cooper, while you were away, we came up with a solution

56
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:51,260
that would allow you to change your field of study.

57
00:02:52,670 --> 00:02:55,650
Pouting and running away actually worked.

58
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,050
Yeah, I must say, that may not be a lesson

59
00:02:58,050 --> 00:02:59,800
you want to reinforce with me.

60
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:03,600
So, um, what is the solution?

61
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:05,690
Currently, you're being paid under a grant

62
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:07,720
to specifically research string theory.

63
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,030
If we promote you to junior professor,

64
00:03:10,030 --> 00:03:11,020
you'll be able to choose

65
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:13,010
whatever field of research you'd like.

66
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:18,340
But if I'm a professor, then I'll have to teach a class.

67
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:19,660
That is correct.

68
00:03:20,990 --> 00:03:25,620
So your solution is to promote me and pay me more money

69
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:27,560
so that I can impart my knowledge

70
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:29,640
to the next generation of scientists?

71
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:30,700
Yes.

72
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:33,950
You people are sick.

73
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:40,670
Hey. Ready to go to lunch?

74
00:03:40,910 --> 00:03:41,710
Just give me a minute.

75
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:44,100
I'm stimulating the pleasure cells of this starfish.

76
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:45,270
I just need to turn it off.

77
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:46,470
What happens if you don't?

78
00:03:46,470 --> 00:03:47,640
Then I have to sit through lunch

79
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:50,350
knowing this starfish is having a better day than I am.

80
00:03:52,510 --> 00:03:54,410
Is Bernadette meeting us at the restaurant?

81
00:03:54,410 --> 00:03:57,350
Uh, no. Actually, I didn't invite her.

82
00:03:57,390 --> 00:03:58,250
How come?

83
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:00,290
Well, ever since she helped me get this job,

84
00:04:00,290 --> 00:04:01,990
she won't stop bugging me.

85
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:04,280
Well, I think she just wants you to do well,

86
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,750
and she's worried that you won't

87
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:09,200
'cause you were just a stunningly poor waitress.

88
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:11,410
That is not true.

89
00:04:11,410 --> 00:04:14,700
I'm still waiting on my mini corndogs from two years ago.

90
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:18,440
I told you, they'll be right out.

91
00:04:19,870 --> 00:04:22,030
Okay, so no Bernadette.

92
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:24,700
Well, you saw her the other night. Am I wrong?

93
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,240
No, I just...

94
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:29,260
I feel kind of uncomfortable talking about her like this.

95
00:04:29,260 --> 00:04:31,700
Usually when someone's being talked about behind their back,

96
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,500
it's me and it's right in front of my face.

97
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:38,010
I-I'm sorry. I just need a little break from her.

98
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:39,330
I understand.

99
00:04:40,290 --> 00:04:43,990
You know, there is some research that indicates

100
00:04:43,990 --> 00:04:47,100
that sharing negative attitudes about a third party

101
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,290
can be a powerful bonding force between two friends.

102
00:04:51,730 --> 00:04:52,890
So, what are you saying?

103
00:04:52,890 --> 00:04:55,690
I'm saying, in the spirit of science,

104
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,470
what is that little skank's problem?

105
00:05:01,890 --> 00:05:03,860
So, I've been trying to come up

106
00:05:03,860 --> 00:05:06,360
with a cute couple's nickname for me and Emily.

107
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:07,490
What do you like better--

108
00:05:07,830 --> 00:05:09,000
Emippali...

109
00:05:10,530 --> 00:05:12,330
or Koothrapemily?

110
00:05:14,270 --> 00:05:17,250
Why is it your last name and her first name?

111
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:18,780
Oh, well, her last name is Sweeney,

112
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:22,350
and something just didn't seem right about Koothrapeeney.

113
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:28,320
Hey, how'd it go with human resources?

114
00:05:28,490 --> 00:05:29,530
Awful.

115
00:05:29,530 --> 00:05:31,920
Yeah, they're allowing me to move on from string theory,

116
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:33,750
but they made me a junior professor

117
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:35,980
and are requiring me to teach a class.

118
00:05:36,090 --> 00:05:38,850
I don't understand. Why is it bad that you have to teach?

119
00:05:38,860 --> 00:05:41,110
What a stupid question.

120
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:44,360
The kids are gonna love him.

121
00:05:45,210 --> 00:05:47,490
I can't believe I have to waste my time

122
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:49,580
babysitting a bunch of grad students

123
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:52,460
who probably think dark matter is what's in their diapers.

124
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:55,450
This might not be that bad.

125
00:05:55,450 --> 00:05:57,320
Uh, you like telling people they're wrong.

126
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:58,400
Wrong!

127
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:02,820
Just because I enjoyed that one doesn't mean I always do.

128
00:06:03,770 --> 00:06:05,750
You enjoy giving people grades.

129
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:09,220
A valid point, but unoriginal-- B-minus.

130
00:06:10,110 --> 00:06:12,010
And you love the sound of your own voice.

131
00:06:12,010 --> 00:06:13,670
Yeah, well, of course I do.

132
00:06:13,670 --> 00:06:14,680
Listen to it.

133
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:17,510
It's like an earful of melted caramel.

134
00:06:18,980 --> 00:06:20,460
Look, most importantly,

135
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:23,240
this will let you move on and study dark matter.

136
00:06:24,270 --> 00:06:27,290
It is true that many of my heroes have taken students

137
00:06:27,290 --> 00:06:28,470
under their wings:

138
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:31,980
Feynman, Einstein, Professor X.

139
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:34,240
Humorously,

140
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:35,670
in the case of Professor X,

141
00:06:35,670 --> 00:06:38,240
some of his students actually had wings.

142
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,690
That's rich.

143
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:43,600
I'll use that one to lighten the mood

144
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:46,360
after my entire class fails the midterm.

145
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:53,450
Measuring starfish serotonin levels in response to

146
00:06:53,450 --> 00:06:57,510
one point two molar stimulation of pleasure cells.

147
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:00,730
You like that, don't you?

148
00:07:02,170 --> 00:07:03,530
That's right, say my name.

149
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:11,740
- Hey, what's going on? - Not much.

150
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:13,070
You want to get a drink later?

151
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:14,250
Just the two of us?

152
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:15,210
No Penny?

153
00:07:15,210 --> 00:07:17,680
Not tonight. I'm a little frustrated with her.

154
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:19,300
Because you got her the job and you think

155
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:21,460
she should be working harder to prepare for it?

156
00:07:21,610 --> 00:07:24,170
So it's not just me. You see it, too!

157
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:28,350
I do, I see it.

158
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:30,950
It's driving me crazy.

159
00:07:30,950 --> 00:07:33,030
Just this afternoon, I saw on Instagram

160
00:07:33,030 --> 00:07:35,290
that instead of studying, she went out to lunch

161
00:07:35,290 --> 00:07:37,050
and got a manicure.

162
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:39,930
That's outrageous!

163
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:41,760
I know!

164
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:42,960
If she doesn't do well,

165
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,360
th-this could reflect poorly on you.

166
00:07:45,730 --> 00:07:49,100
Exactly. Does she not realize it or does she not care?

167
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:50,350
I don't know.

168
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:52,830
The important thing is I am here for you

169
00:07:52,830 --> 00:07:56,860
so we can mutually disparage this unpleasing third party.

170
00:08:02,970 --> 00:08:05,670
Hey. We just wanted to see how your class was going.

171
00:08:05,670 --> 00:08:07,050
Where is everybody?

172
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:08,990
There is no class.

173
00:08:08,990 --> 00:08:11,970
Did you send everyone to the principal's office already?

174
00:08:13,810 --> 00:08:15,450
No one signed up.

175
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:17,640
Well, that's not your fault.

176
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:20,620
I called the department secretary to see what happened.

177
00:08:20,620 --> 00:08:24,320
Apparently, I have a reputation for being obnoxious.

178
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:27,950
What?

179
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:31,090
Hey, Sheldon, I'm sorry.

180
00:08:31,090 --> 00:08:33,640
No, it's fine.

181
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:37,400
Now I can devote all my time to dark matter.

182
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:39,650
Aw, you brought cookies for everyone?

183
00:08:39,650 --> 00:08:41,680
Oh, yes. Fig Newtons.

184
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:44,700
I was going to ask them: which scientist both helped

185
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:46,550
to develop calculus

186
00:08:46,550 --> 00:08:48,230
and had a famous cookie named after him?

187
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:50,030
And then after someone said "Newton,"

188
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:52,000
I was going to tell them they're wrong.

189
00:08:53,110 --> 00:08:56,180
The cookies are named after a town in Massachusetts.

190
00:08:56,830 --> 00:08:58,840
And then I'd throw the cookies away.

191
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:03,150
Hey, what if I took your class?

192
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:05,790
Why would you do that?

193
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:07,630
- Yeah, why would you do that? - What's wrong with you?

194
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:10,930
I'm thinking about getting my doctorate,

195
00:09:10,930 --> 00:09:12,440
and he wants to teach-- why not?

196
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:14,280
Oh, Howard.

197
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:15,970
I appreciate the gesture,

198
00:09:15,970 --> 00:09:19,090
but this is a graduate-level physics class.

199
00:09:20,930 --> 00:09:21,890
I don't think you'd understand

200
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:24,130
a single thing I was talking about.

201
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:28,300
Ask why not again; I've got an answer.

202
00:09:29,350 --> 00:09:32,470
Sheldon, I'm more than smart enough to take your class.

203
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:35,930
No.

204
00:09:38,250 --> 00:09:39,430
Yes.

205
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:43,110
How would you determine the ground state

206
00:09:43,110 --> 00:09:46,060
of a quantum system with no exact solution?

207
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:48,520
I would guess a wave-function and then vary its parameters

208
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:50,870
until I found the lowest energy solution.

209
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:56,340
Do you know how to integrate X squared times E

210
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:58,560
to the minus X, without looking it up?

211
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:00,330
I'd use Feynman's trick--

212
00:10:00,330 --> 00:10:02,200
differentiate under the integral sign.

213
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:04,040
Okay.

214
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:10,500
What is the correct interpretation of quantum mechanics?

215
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:12,600
Since every interpretation gives exactly the same answer

216
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:14,350
to every measurement, they are all equally correct.

217
00:10:14,350 --> 00:10:16,680
However, I know you believe in the Many Worlds Interpretation,

218
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:19,890
so I'll say that. Now do you think I'm smart enough?

219
00:10:23,070 --> 00:10:24,380
No.

220
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:27,790
Oh, come on. You might've gone to school

221
00:10:27,790 --> 00:10:29,680
for a couple more years than me, but guess what--

222
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:32,210
engineers are just as smart as physicists.

223
00:10:32,210 --> 00:10:34,060
You take that back!

224
00:10:37,570 --> 00:10:39,080
No.

225
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:46,310
So, after drinks with Bernadette,

226
00:10:46,310 --> 00:10:49,300
I get home, and Penny calls to complain about her.

227
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:50,380
And then while I'm talking to Penny,

228
00:10:50,380 --> 00:10:51,840
I get a text from Bernadette.

229
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:54,890
Wh... I'm trying to prepare my lesson plan for Howard.

230
00:10:54,900 --> 00:10:56,730
Why are you telling me this?

231
00:10:57,870 --> 00:10:59,390
Because it's taken 15 years,

232
00:10:59,390 --> 00:11:02,080
but high school is finally awesome!

233
00:11:04,410 --> 00:11:05,480
I love them both,

234
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:08,910
but I'm in the center now, and I love that even more.

235
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:12,250
Amy, please. I am trying to figure out a way

236
00:11:12,250 --> 00:11:15,770
to intellectually emasculate a dear friend of mine.

237
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:18,550
- But I'm just... - Not now.

238
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:20,210
You better watch that attitude, buddy.

239
00:11:20,210 --> 00:11:22,280
You're dating the popular girl now.

240
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:28,450
You're up late.

241
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:31,370
Oh, I'm working on my lesson plan for Wolowitz.

242
00:11:31,370 --> 00:11:33,440
He is going to be so lost.

243
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:35,770
Look at this section over here.

244
00:11:36,310 --> 00:11:38,290
Even I don't really understand it.

245
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:42,630
Sheldon, why are you doing this?

246
00:11:42,630 --> 00:11:44,160
I'm a teacher, Leonard.

247
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:45,490
It's my job.

248
00:11:46,650 --> 00:11:47,640
No, I-I mean,

249
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:49,030
why are you going to so much trouble

250
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:50,990
to prove that you're smarter than Wolowitz?

251
00:11:50,990 --> 00:11:53,480
Oh, it's no trouble; it's actually a pleasure.

252
00:11:55,490 --> 00:11:56,830
You want to know what I think?

253
00:11:57,090 --> 00:12:00,170
I think the idea that someone could be as smart as you,

254
00:12:00,170 --> 00:12:02,970
or even smarter, scares the pants off you,

255
00:12:02,970 --> 00:12:04,600
and you can't deal with it.

256
00:12:06,530 --> 00:12:08,110
Interesting point.

257
00:12:08,590 --> 00:12:10,080
Wh... You're suggesting

258
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:12,350
that I have emotional issues below my consciousness

259
00:12:12,350 --> 00:12:14,220
which drive my behavior,

260
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:17,360
thus causing me to lash out at anything or anyone

261
00:12:17,360 --> 00:12:20,350
that threatens my intellectual superiority.

262
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:22,620
Might be something to think about.

263
00:12:24,420 --> 00:12:25,550
- Leonard? - Yeah.

264
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:28,080
Howard's allergic to peanuts.

265
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:29,760
How can I use that against him?

266
00:12:35,860 --> 00:12:39,270
Okay, now that everyone's here, we can begin.

267
00:12:41,770 --> 00:12:44,020
Before we do, I just talked to Leonard.

268
00:12:44,020 --> 00:12:45,470
And if you're gonna

